# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this, Windows git's default `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites LF →
# CRLF on checkout for any text file, which breaks shell scripts the
# moment WSL bash (or any POSIX shell) tries to execute them — every
# line ends with a stray \r and the parser chokes on it.
#
# Pin each kind of script to its required line-ending convention so a
# fresh checkout on any platform Just Works.

# POSIX shell + macOS double-clickables — must be LF.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
*.command   text eol=lf

# Windows batch files — cmd.exe requires CRLF.
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# PowerShell — CRLF is the Windows convention; pin so Windows checkouts
# don't get LF when something else's `text=auto` rule gets cleverer.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.psm1      text eol=crlf
